  Now let me pin down the task core. Four things left to settle: who this is for, how tasks are shared, the stack, and
  how rich the task model is. Starting with the one that colors the other three.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how many people and how much trust. This decides whether auth is a checkbox or a project, and whether the
  data model needs tenancy boundaries.

  - [1mSmall trusted group[0m — everyone sees everything; permissions are near-trivial, auth can be a single shared identity
    provider or even simple sessions. Cheap. Rules out selling it later without a real tenancy retrofit, which is a
    painful migration once data exists.
  - [1mA real product with separate organizations[0m — tenancy in the schema from day one, proper auth, per-org isolation.
    Expensive up front, but tenancy is the single hardest thing to add to a live database later.
  - [1mOpen/public collaboration[0m — anyone can sign up and be invited to tasks; no org boundary, permissions are
    per-resource. Middle cost, different shape entirely.

  [1mMy recommendation depends on your honest answer, not the ambitious one.[0m If this is a tool for a team you know, say so
  — I'll keep the permission model thin and you'll ship far sooner. Tenancy is the one thing I'd argue for adding early
  [3monly[0m if there's a genuine chance of multiple organizations, because retrofitting it is a data migration rather than a
  code change.
